    title: "信韓銀行客戶資料外洩：因缺乏基本權限驗證與API控制導致的資安事件分析",
    summary: "信韓銀行（Shinhan Bank）發生客戶資料外洩事件，約有 2.5 萬名客戶的貸款相關資訊遭到竊取。此次事件的嚴重性在於，外洩的資料包含年收入、貸款額度等高度敏感的個人金融資產資訊，甚至包括個人識別碼（CI）和居民註冊號碼，極易被攻擊者用於二次詐騙，如語音詐騙（Voice Phishing）或精準釣魚（Spear Phishing）。\n\n資安專家指出，此次攻擊並非高階的持續性威脅（APT），而是源於銀行系統「基本安全機制」的缺失。初步入侵路徑被懷疑是用於查詢貸款申請進度的行動服務「M Shinhan」。攻擊者利用從暗網獲取的帳號資訊進行「帳密填充」（Credential Stuffing），並透過操縱客戶號碼，對一般客戶查詢服務的 API 進行重複請求，從而大量爬取數據。\n\n核心問題在於，系統缺乏足夠的「速率限制」（Rate Limiting）和「頻率限制」（Throttling）來限制登入嘗試次數，更關鍵的是，在「認證」（Authentication）成功後，缺乏伺服器端的「授權」（Authorization）交叉驗證機制，導致發生典型的「權限控制缺陷」（Broken Access Control）。專家建議，金融機構必須在外部網路與內部網路之間建立嚴格的 DMZ 監控，並實施即時的「運行時防禦」（Runtime Defense）系統，以防止異常的 API 呼叫。\n\n信韓銀行已採取緊急措施，包括封鎖外部 IP 和中斷相關服務，並設立專門諮詢中心，承諾對因資料外洩造成的實際金融損失進行全額賠償。",
    tags: ["信韓銀行", "Credential Stuffing", "API 權限控制缺陷", "Broken Access Control", "DMZ", "資安事件"],
    title_en: "Shinhan Bank Customer Data Leak: Analysis of Security Incident Caused by Lack of Basic Authorization and API Control",
    summary_en: "Shinhan Bank experienced a customer data leak, resulting in the theft of loan-related information belonging to approximately 25,000 customers. The severity of this incident lies in the fact that the leaked data includes highly sensitive personal financial assets, such as annual income and loan limits, and even personal identification codes (CI) and resident registration numbers, making it highly susceptible to being used by attackers for secondary fraud, such as Voice Phishing or Spear Phishing.\n\nCybersecurity experts point out that this attack was not a sophisticated Advanced Persistent Threat (APT), but rather stemmed from the bank system's lack of 'basic security mechanisms.' The initial intrusion path is suspected to involve the mobile service 'M Shinhan,' which is used for checking loan application progress. Attackers utilized account information obtained from the dark web to perform 'Credential Stuffing,' and by manipulating customer numbers, they repeatedly requested the API for general customer inquiry services, thereby scraping large amounts of data.\n\nThe core problem is the system's lack of sufficient 'Rate Limiting' and 'Throttling' to restrict login attempts, and more critically, the absence of server-side 'Authorization' cross-validation mechanisms after successful 'Authentication,' leading to a typical 'Broken Access Control' vulnerability. Experts recommend that financial institutions must establish strict DMZ monitoring between the external and internal networks, and implement real-time 'Runtime Defense' systems to prevent anomalous API calls.\n\nShinhan Bank has taken emergency measures, including blocking external IPs and suspending related services, and has established a dedicated consultation center, promising full compensation for any actual financial losses resulting from the data leak.",
